{"name":"Pearls & Lace","hex":"#dcd0cb","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pearls","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of pearl."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of pearl."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pearls"},{"term":"lace","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light fabric containing patterns of holes, usually built up from a single thread.","A cord or ribbon passed through eyelets in a shoe or garment, pulled tight and tied to fasten the shoe or garment firmly."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fasten (something) with laces.","To interweave items."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lace"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pearl","blocks":[{"text":"A pearl is a hard, shiny object produced within the soft tissue of a living shelled mollusk. Like the shell of a mollusk, a pearl is composed of calcium carbonate in a crystalline form, which is deposited in concentric layers. More commercially valuable pearls are perfectly round and smooth, but many other shapes, known as baroque pearls, can occur. The highest quality of natural pearls have been highly valued as gemstones and objects of beauty for many centuries. In literature, pearls have become a metaphor for something rare, fine, admirable, and valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/330px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl"},{"title":"Lace","blocks":[{"text":"Lace is a decorative openwork textile made without the use of pre-existing fabric. Lace can be made by hand or machine. Various techniques can be employed to form lace, each having a distinct category. Lace can also be used to refer to other types of openwork fabric such as cutwork or filet, which use pre-existing fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG/330px-Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lace"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/500px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/500px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVarious_pearls.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/500px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/1280px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/500px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Georgian seed pearl gold ring","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeorgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/500px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/1280px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/500px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A black pearl and a shell of the black-lipped pearl oyster. The iridescent colors originate from nacre layers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Nacre_microscopic_structure.png/500px-Nacre_microscopic_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Nacre_microscopic_structure.png/500px-Nacre_microscopic_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of nacre layers, wherein aragonite plates are separated by biopolymers, such as chitin, lustrin and silk-like proteins","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANacre_microscopic_structure.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG/500px-Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG/500px-Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Electron microscopy image of a fractured surface of nacre","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf/page1-500px-PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf/page1-1280px-PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf/page1-500px-PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Formation of non–bead-cultured Akoya \"keshi\" pearls produced in a P. i. fucata mollusk. (A) Optical overview of a nonbeaded keshi–cultured pearl. (B) Cross-section showing CaCO3 growth begins onto an organic center. (C) Mature nacre. (D and E) Atomic-resolution image of atoms in nacre. (F) Transition from spherulitic aragonite structures to nacre. (G and H) Aggregated nanoparticles form massive aragonite. (I) The formation of nacre begins directly on massive aragonite.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg/500px-Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg/500px-Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two halved river pearls, natural freshwater pearls, on a slab of lapis lazuli. The pearls are about 2.5–3 mm (0.098–0.118 in) in diameter. The growth layers are clearly visible.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwo_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Pearls.jpg/500px-Pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Pearls.jpg/500px-Pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nuclei from Mikimoto Pearl Island, Toba, Japan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APearls.jpg"}],"sounds":{"lace":[{